---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import RedoxSim from '../../simulators/redox/RedoxSim.astro';
import { redoxQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/redox';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What is a redox reaction?' },
  { id: 'agents', label: 'Oxidizing and reducing agents' },
  { id: 'states', label: 'Oxidation states' },
  { id: 'half', label: 'Half-equations' },
  { id: 'shared', label: 'Transferred or shared electrons?' },
  { id: 'reactivity', label: 'Reactivity and displacement' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 4.2: Classifying Chemical Reactions (Oxidation-Reduction Reactions). OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-2-classifying-chemical-reactions' },
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 17.1: Review of Redox Chemistry. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-1-review-of-redox-chemistry' },
  { text: 'IUPAC. Compendium of Chemical Terminology (the “Gold Book”): oxidation state.', url: 'https://goldbook.iupac.org/' },
  { text: 'Royal Society of Chemistry. Displacement reactions between metals and their aqueous ions; halogen displacement reactions (Education practical resources).', url: 'https://edu.rsc.org/' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="chemistry"
  topic="redox-reactions"
  description="Interactive redox simulator: watch electrons move from reducing agent to oxidizing agent in six real reactions, track oxidation states, write half-equations and test yourself."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={redoxQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Rusting iron, a burning match, the battery in your phone and the way your cells release energy from food all have
      one thing in common: electrons moving from one substance to another. Step through six real reactions, follow every
      electron, and learn to spot which substance is oxidized and which is reduced.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <RedoxSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>reaction</strong>. Read what you would see in the lab in the box below the simulator.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong> to play the reaction, or use the stage buttons: <strong>1 Reactants</strong>, <strong>2 Electron transfer</strong> and <strong>3 Products</strong>.</li>
      <li>Watch the <strong>oxidation states</strong> above each particle. In the products, green ↑ means oxidized and red ↓ means reduced. The table below the simulator lists every change.</li>
      <li>Click any particle for an explanation. Compare <strong>Hydrogen burning in oxygen</strong> with the others: its electrons are shared, not handed over.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Test yourself</strong> to hide the answers, then identify what is oxidized, what is reduced and the two agents. <strong>Reset</strong> restores the first reaction.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What is a redox reaction?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Redox</strong> is short for <strong>red</strong>uction–<strong>ox</strong>idation. Originally, oxidation meant
    combining with oxygen, as when magnesium burns to magnesium oxide. Today chemists define it by electrons:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Oxidation</strong> is the <strong>loss</strong> of electrons.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduction</strong> is the <strong>gain</strong> of electrons.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A handy memory aid is <strong>OIL RIG</strong>: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain. The two always happen together:
    electrons lost by one substance must be gained by another, so the electrons lost always equal the electrons gained.
  </p>

  <h2 id="agents">Oxidizing and reducing agents</h2>
  <p>
    The substance that <strong>gives</strong> electrons is the <strong>reducing agent</strong>: it reduces something else
    and is itself oxidized. The substance that <strong>takes</strong> electrons is the <strong>oxidizing agent</strong>: it
    oxidizes something else and is itself reduced. In zinc plus copper(II) sulfate, zinc is the reducing agent and copper(II)
    ions are the oxidizing agent. Oxygen, chlorine and many metal ions are common oxidizing agents; reactive metals and
    hydrogen are common reducing agents.
  </p>

  <h2 id="states">Oxidation states</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>oxidation state</strong> (or oxidation number) is the charge an atom would have if all its bonds were
    ionic. It lets you track electrons in any reaction. The main rules:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>An uncombined element is 0 (Zn, O₂, Cl₂).</li>
    <li>A simple ion equals its charge (Na⁺ is +1, O²⁻ is −2).</li>
    <li>Oxygen is usually −2 and hydrogen is usually +1 in compounds.</li>
    <li>The oxidation states in a compound add up to zero, and in an ion they add up to its charge.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If an element’s oxidation state <strong>goes up</strong>, it has been oxidized; if it <strong>goes down</strong>, it has
    been reduced. If nothing changes, the reaction is not redox. For example, sulfur in SO₄²⁻ is +6, because
    x + 4(−2) = −2.
  </p>

  <h2 id="half">Half-equations</h2>
  <p>
    A redox reaction can be split into two <strong>half-equations</strong> that show the electrons explicitly. For zinc and
    copper(II) ions:
  </p>
  <code class="formula">Oxidation: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻</code>
  <code class="formula">Reduction: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</code>
  <p>
    Adding them, the electrons cancel: Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu. If the numbers of electrons differ, multiply one
    half-equation first. That is why copper needs two silver ions: Cu loses 2 electrons, and each Ag⁺ gains only one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="shared">Transferred or shared electrons?</h2>
  <p>
    In metal displacement and in reactions that make ionic compounds, electrons really do move from one particle to
    another. Zinc atoms on the surface of a zinc strip pass electrons to copper ions that touch them. But when hydrogen
    burns to make water, the product is covalent: the electrons are <strong>shared</strong>, unequally, with oxygen pulling
    them closer because it is more electronegative. Oxidation states treat those shared electrons as if they belonged
    entirely to oxygen. This bookkeeping is extremely useful, but it is not a picture of electrons flying from hydrogen to
    oxygen. The simulator shows the shift of shared pairs instead.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reactivity">Reactivity and displacement</h2>
  <p>
    A more reactive metal gives up electrons more readily, so it can reduce the ions of a less reactive metal:
    zinc displaces copper, and copper displaces silver. The reverse reactions do not happen. Among the halogens, the more
    reactive one is the stronger oxidizing agent: chlorine oxidizes bromide ions to bromine, but bromine cannot oxidize
    chloride ions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Oxidation</dt>
    <dd>Loss of electrons; an increase in oxidation state.</dd>
    <dt>Reduction</dt>
    <dd>Gain of electrons; a decrease in oxidation state.</dd>
    <dt>Oxidizing agent</dt>
    <dd>The species that accepts electrons and is reduced.</dd>
    <dt>Reducing agent</dt>
    <dd>The species that donates electrons and is oxidized.</dd>
    <dt>Spectator ion</dt>
    <dd>An ion present in the reaction mixture that does not change.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Batteries</strong> separate the oxidation and reduction half-reactions so that the electrons flow through your device.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrosion:</strong> iron is oxidized by oxygen and water to form rust.</li>
    <li><strong>Extracting metals:</strong> iron ore is reduced to iron in a blast furnace.</li>
    <li><strong>Bleach and disinfectants</strong> are oxidizing agents that destroy coloured compounds and microbes.</li>
    <li><strong>Respiration and photosynthesis</strong> are chains of redox reactions in living cells.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Oxidation always involves oxygen.”</strong> Sodium is oxidized by chlorine with no oxygen present.</li>
    <li><strong>“The oxidizing agent is oxidized.”</strong> It is reduced; it oxidizes something else.</li>
    <li><strong>“Oxidation and reduction can happen on their own.”</strong> They always happen together.</li>
    <li><strong>“Oxidation states are real charges.”</strong> Only for simple ions; in covalent molecules they are bookkeeping.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The simulator shows a handful of particles from each reaction, drawn as circles whose sizes only roughly reflect that
    atoms shrink when they lose electrons and grow when they gain them. Real reactions involve enormous numbers of
    particles: the ionic products form crystal lattices, metal atoms are part of a solid surface, and ions in solution
    are surrounded by water molecules. Electrons are drawn as dots travelling along a curved path to show where they
    end up, not the actual route they take. Spectator ions are shown faded. For the hydrogen–oxygen reaction, the
    half-equations are formal: in a flame the reaction happens through many fast steps involving reactive fragments, and
    the half-reactions only happen separately in a device such as a hydrogen fuel cell.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Oxidation is loss of electrons; reduction is gain of electrons (OIL RIG). They always happen together.</li>
    <li>The reducing agent is oxidized; the oxidizing agent is reduced.</li>
    <li>Oxidation states track electrons: up means oxidized, down means reduced, no change means not redox.</li>
    <li>Half-equations show the electrons; electrons lost must equal electrons gained.</li>
    <li>In covalent products, electrons are shared unequally rather than fully transferred.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
